---
import type { GetStaticPaths } from 'astro';
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';
import { Picture, getImage } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Plate from '../../components/Plate.astro';
import {
  bodyWidth,
  bodySizes,
  EXPANDED_WIDTH,
  FRAME_FORMATS,
  FRAME_FALLBACK,
} from '../../layout';
import { PERSON_ID } from '../../site';

export const getStaticPaths = (async () => {
  const projects = await getCollection('projects');
  return projects.map((project) => ({ params: { slug: project.id }, props: { project } }));
}) satisfies GetStaticPaths;

const { project } = Astro.props;
const { title, summary, credit, spec, year, images } = project.data;
const { Content } = await render(project);

/* Frames 1..n, then the hero again LAST and at its native aspect.

   It is not a repeat. The thing at the top of this page is a 21:9 band cut out
   of the hero, and closing the sequence with the whole image is what that band
   was taken from — the crop opens the page, the full frame resolves it. Last
   rather than first because first would put it directly under its own crop,
   with only the prose between, which reads as the image failing to load rather
   than as a sequence.

   `images` is `.min(1)` in the schema, so this is never empty. A one-image
   Project is exactly its hero shown uncropped, which is the right answer. */
const frames = [...images.slice(1), images[0]];

/* The Expanded View's source, one per Frame, resolved at build time so the
   markup can be a plain link.

   `layout: 'none'` for the reason Base.astro spells out on the Social Card:
   astro.config.mjs sets a global 'constrained' default that applies to getImage
   too, and without this each of these becomes a six-width srcset where exactly
   one URL is read.

   Clamped to the source width so a portrait Frame is never upscaled. Every
   committed asset is 3200 px on its LONG edge (docs/asset-delivery.md §2), so a
   tall one can be well under 2000 px wide and sharp would happily enlarge it. */
const expanded = await Promise.all(
  frames.map((frame) =>
    getImage({
      src: frame.src,
      width: Math.min(EXPANDED_WIDTH, frame.src.width),
      format: 'webp',
      layout: 'none',
    }),
  ),
);

/* THE PROJECT AS AN ENTITY. See docs/content-architecture.md §7 and ADR-0012,
   which records that this reverses a written refusal and what changed.

   EVERY FIELD HERE IS RENDERED BY THIS PAGE. That is the whole licence for the
   graph existing: content-architecture.md's governing rule is that a field no
   page renders does not exist, and the refusal this replaces was correct while
   no route rendered a Project. `title`, `summary` and `year` are all on screen
   below, and the hero is the first thing on it.

   `spec` AND `credit` ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and their absence is the part
   most likely to be "fixed" by someone later. Neither has an honest schema.org
   field. The Specification Line is a toolchain, and nothing in the vocabulary
   means that — `material` is about physical substance. The Credit is provenance
   prose whose only plausible home is `sourceOrganization`, which asserts an
   organization edge; ADR-0005 forbids that and check-structured-data.mjs now
   enforces the neighbouring half of the same rule. A graph that carries only
   what maps cleanly is the point, not a gap in it.

   `creator` IS A REFERENCE, NEVER A NESTED PERSON. Six pages each embedding
   their own copy of the person is six unconnected people to a crawler, and six
   places to change one fact. The node is defined once on `/`.

   `dateCreated` TAKES A BARE YEAR because `year` is an int and a bare year is
   valid ISO 8601. Do not pad it to a January date to look more precise — the
   schema holds a year because a year is what is known, and `2024-01-01` would
   assert a day nobody stated.

   `image` REUSES THE EXPANDED VIEW'S ENCODE rather than making its own. The
   hero is `frames`' LAST entry (see the comment on `frames` above), so it is
   `expanded.at(-1)` — an already-emitted 2000px webp. Calling getImage again
   here with different arguments would add a seventh variant per Project to a
   build that astro.config.mjs's comments describe as cost-dominated by variant
   count, in exchange for an image no human ever sees. */
const canonical = new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site);
const work = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'CreativeWork',
  '@id': `${canonical}#work`,
  name: title,
  description: summary,
  url: canonical.href,
  dateCreated: String(year),
  image: new URL(expanded.at(-1)!.src, Astro.site).href,
  creator: { '@id': PERSON_ID },
};
---

{/* THE EYEBROW IS THE PROJECT'S NAME, not the word `Project`, and the reason is
    a measurement rather than a preference. On a 390x844 phone the Masthead is
    786px of an 844px screen and it is IDENTICAL on every route but for this one
    string, so a Project page's first screen was `/`'s first screen: same name,
    same role, same lede, same specification line, same portrait, same About
    band. Then the first thing under <main> is the hero, which on `/` is the same
    file at the same crop. The page's own <h2> does not appear until y=1331,
    1.58 screens down. Arriving here from a Plate read as the index reloading.

    `Project` was the one fact a visitor could already infer — they had just
    tapped a Plate — and the name was the one they could not. See the amendment
    on docs/adr/0011.

    IT STAYS THE EYEBROW rather than becoming a second heading. ADR-0011's rule
    is that exactly one thing varies per route and it is this string; what
    changed is what the string says, not how many things say it. `.t-label`
    uppercases, and casing is part of the type style (tokens.css) — so this
    renders CECRET, not Cecret, with no `text-transform` anywhere. */}
<Base
  title={`${title} | Coulter Heiberger`}
  description={summary}
  eyebrow={title}
  image={images[0]}
  structuredData={work}
>
  {/* The hero leads the PAGE'S OWN CONTENT. This page IS the Plate in its
      second presentation, and putting the work first is the same argument
      content-architecture.md §1 makes for `/` not being a landing page.

      IT NO LONGER LEADS THE DOCUMENT, and that is a deliberate reversal rather
      than an oversight. This comment used to end: "It also keeps the page
      image-LCP, which is the regime ADR-0002 costed the ~220 KB hero crossover
      in. Three hundred pixels of heading above it would hand the LCP to an <h1>
      on mobile and quietly move the page into the other regime." That is exactly
      what ADR-0011 did, on purpose: the Masthead is site furniture now and sits
      above this in Base.astro, so on a phone the LCP element is its positioning
      paragraph and this page is text-LCP.

      "QUIETLY" WAS THE OBJECTION, AND IT IS THE PART THAT WAS ANSWERED. The move
      was measured before it shipped and the numbers are in ADR-0011. `/` had
      already made the same shift when the Masthead landed there — index.astro
      carries those measurements — so this is the eight other routes joining a
      regime one route was already in, not a new one being entered blind.

      WHAT STILL HOLDS: ADR-0002's ~220 KB crossover and lighthouserc.cjs's
      hero-bytes table were both derived with the hero as the LCP element, and
      they still describe the landscape case, where it is. */}
  {/* REGISTRATION, TIER A: the first screen animates from the stylesheet with
      no script on the path, so the hero can start on the first frame. It is
      the LCP element on this page — the hero leads precisely to keep the page
      image-LCP — so it carries NO --d. An animation-delay on the LCP element
      is added to LCP one for one (ADR-0010).

      THE OTHER THREE ARE THE GROUP OF FOUR the design spec names, at the
      site's 60ms unit: title, summary, facts. The three facts are one <dl>
      and register together rather than as three, which is ADR-0009's argument
      about the metadata band applied to the same kind of content — it is one
      datum arriving, not three.

      The Plate wrapper carries data-anim, never the <Plate> itself: a
      component's scoped `transition` shorthand replaces one inherited from
      base.css at higher specificity, which is finding #1 in docs/motion.md
      and looks exactly like a page with no animation. */}
  <div class="hero" data-anim="now">
    <Plate frame={images[0]} priority />
  </div>

  <h2 data-anim="now" style="--d: calc(1 * var(--reveal-stagger))">{title}</h2>
  <p class="summary" data-anim="now" style="--d: calc(2 * var(--reveal-stagger))">{summary}</p>

  {/* Credit and the Specification Line together — provenance and
      construction, the pair CONTEXT.md defines against each other. `credit`
      appears here and nowhere else on the site (content-architecture.md §2). */}
  <dl class="facts" data-anim="now" style="--d: calc(3 * var(--reveal-stagger))">
    <div>
      <dt class="t-label">Credit</dt>
      <dd class="t-spec">{credit}</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt class="t-label">Year</dt>
      <dd class="t-spec">{year}</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt class="t-label">Specification</dt>
      <dd class="t-spec">{spec}</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  {/* TIER B FROM HERE DOWN: below the fold, so these start hidden and the
      observer in Base.astro un-hides each one once as it comes into view.

      THE WRAPPER REGISTERS, NOT THE PARAGRAPHS. The body is MDX and its
      children cannot be marked in this template, but that is not why: it is
      one block of writing arriving, and registering it line by line is the
      performance the direction refuses. Same argument as the <dl> above. */}
  <div class="prose" data-anim="scroll">
    <Content />
  </div>

  {/* The gallery. Frames at NATIVE ASPECT — no crop, no `framing`, because
      nothing here is being fitted to a band: the two art-directed crops exist
      for the Plate, which has a layout to satisfy, and a Frame does not.

      No entrance on any of them, and none is coming back by default. An image
      the visitor scrolled to is not information arriving, it is the thing they
      came to see, and twelve of them entering down one column is precisely the
      performance the motion spec was written to refuse. See docs/motion.md.

      No captions and no process/finished label. ~15 of the 47 Frames are
      working images rather than finished camera renders and they render
      identically; the body prose above makes that argument better than a
      badge would. See content-architecture.md §4.

      EACH FRAME IS A LINK TO ITS OWN FULL-SIZE FILE, and Base.astro's script
      intercepts the click into the Expanded View. A link rather than a button
      on purpose: with scripting off, or on a cmd-click, it still does the
      thing a visitor wanted, and its accessible name comes from the alt text
      already on the image inside it. A <button> here would be a control that
      does nothing when the script does not run, and lighthouserc.cjs asserts
      `link-name` and `button-name` alike without being able to tell the
      difference. */}
  <ul class="gallery">
    {frames.map((frame, i) => (
      <li data-anim="scroll">
        <a class="expand" href={expanded[i].src}>
          {/* <Picture>, and here it IS the right tool — which is worth saying
              because Plate.astro argues at length for hand-assembling one.
              That argument is about COMPOSITION: a Plate needs a different
              crop per breakpoint, and <Picture> switches format, not framing.
              A Frame is one crop at one aspect and needs exactly the format
              switch, so the component that does only that is the correct one.

              AVIF WITH A WEBP FALLBACK, where this was WebP alone. The Plate
              pipeline has always led with AVIF and the gallery never did, and
              on mobile that is not a cosmetic gap: `loading="lazy"` does not
              mean "not now" — Chrome's lazy threshold widens as the connection
              slows, so on the throttled 4G the perf gate emulates, the first
              few Frames are fetched WHILE the hero is still arriving and take
              bandwidth from the LCP element. Cecret's terrain map was the
              largest single asset on its page at 199.1 KB, landing at 2.8s.
              Measured through sharp at the width actually served, AVIF q50
              against WebP q80: 199.1 KB -> 93.6 KB, and 122.4 -> 57.4 at the
              rung below. A 53% cut on the thing that was crowding the hero. */}
          <Picture
            src={frame.src}
            alt={frame.alt}
            width={bodyWidth}
            sizes={bodySizes}
            formats={FRAME_FORMATS}
            fallbackFormat={FRAME_FALLBACK}
            layout="constrained"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  {/* ONE dialog per page, not one per Frame. The script sets its src from the
      link that was clicked, so the file is fetched on demand and nothing is
      downloaded for a Frame nobody opens — a Gate in CONTEXT.md's sense.

      The <img> ships with no src and an empty alt, and both are replaced at
      open time. Empty rather than absent because an image with no alt is an
      `image-alt` failure the moment it has a src, and the window between
      those two assignments is not somewhere to be clever.

      Close is a word, not a glyph: it needs no aria-label, it renders in the
      specification line's own type role, and there is no icon set on this
      site for it to be the first member of. */}
  <dialog class="expanded">
    <button type="button" class="t-spec close" data-close>Close</button>
    <img alt="" />
  </dialog>
</Base>

<style>
  /* ONE DATUM EDGE. Everything on this page — hero, heading, facts, prose and
     every Frame — starts on the same left edge, and only the right edges vary.
     That is the literal reading of the direction's own name, and it is what
     makes Cecret's seven near-identical terrain masks read as a sequence rather
     than as seven competing heroes. It is also what gives a 5-Frame Project and
     a 12-Frame Project the same rhythm.

     Recorded honestly: this is a READING of docs/design-direction.md, not a
     measurement. The repo holds no layout geometry from the precedent analysis
     — the prototype is gone (issue #11) — so the only numbers behind it are
     tokens.css and the page shell.

     THE SHELL ITSELF IS NO LONGER DECLARED HERE. `max-width: --measure-page`,
     `margin-inline: auto` and the --gap gutter were restated in this file and in
     three others; base.css states them once now, for every direct child of
     <body>, so the Masthead lands on the same left edge this page argues for
     without a fifth copy. The datum edge is the same edge; it is just no longer
     four files agreeing to draw it. */
  .hero {
    margin-bottom: var(--s5);
  }

  /* ONE COLUMN BELOW THE HERO, AND IT IS THE FULL SHELL. Heading, summary,
     prose and every Frame run the same width the hero does, so the page is one
     column from the top of the image to the last Frame — one left edge and now
     one right edge as well.

     IT USED TO STOP SHORT, at --body-col (800px against a 1280px shell), and
     the argument was rhythm: a narrower body under a wide hero gave a 5-Frame
     and a 12-Frame Project the same shape. That has been traded away on
     purpose. The work is what the page is for and it was being shown at 62.5%
     of the room the page already had. --body-col still exists and still serves
     `/` and /about/ime/; this page no longer reads it.

     Text and Frames still share ONE measure, which is the part that was never
     about the number: two right edges down the page is what the single column
     replaced, and it still does. What the wider measure costs the prose is
     recorded on the --body-col token in tokens.css and is now larger.

     The cap is base.css's --measure-page and the padding is --gap, so nothing
     below needs a max-width of its own. layout.ts composes `bodySizes` from the
     same token, so the width the CSS draws and the width the browser fetches
     cannot drift apart.

     IT IS AN h2 NOW, AND NOTHING MOVED. tokens.css's rule has always been that
     h1 is the site's NAME and h2 is a page's own title; this heading was an h1
     holding --t-h2 because the site's name was not on this page. ADR-0011 put
     the Masthead on every route, so the name is here and this is the page's
     title, which is what it always said it was. The size is the one it already
     had — only the tag changed. */
  h2 {
    font-size: var(--t-h2);
    line-height: 1.02;
  }

  .summary {
    margin: var(--s3) 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .facts {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s2) var(--s5);
    margin: var(--s5) 0 0;
    padding: var(--s3) 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .facts div {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s1);
  }

  .facts dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .prose {
    margin-top: var(--rhythm);
  }

  .gallery {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--rhythm) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .gallery li + li {
    margin-top: var(--s5);
  }

  /* The link is a block wrapper and nothing else. No underline, no border, no
     colour change: base.css gives every <a> `color: inherit` and there is no
     text inside this one to distinguish. The cursor is the affordance. */
  .expand {
    display: block;
  }

  /* <picture> is inline by default, which would put a descender's worth of gap
     under every Frame inside its block-level link. */
  .gallery :global(picture) {
    display: block;
  }

  .gallery :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* ---- the Expanded View -------------------------------------------------
     CONTEXT.md names it. One Frame at the size of the window, over the rest of
     the page blurred back.

     THE BLUR IS ON THE DIALOG, NOT ON ::backdrop, and that is not a stylistic
     choice. Custom properties only began inheriting into ::backdrop recently
     and unevenly, and where they do not, `var(--ground)` resolves to nothing
     and the UA's own translucent black lands over a paper site. A real element
     resolves the token normally in every engine. So the dialog is sized to the
     whole viewport, carries the ground itself, and ::backdrop is cleared out
     from under it.

     `overflow: hidden` because backdrop-filter establishes a containing block
     and a large Frame must not be able to scroll the page underneath. */
  dialog.expanded {
    width: 100vw;
    height: 100dvh;
    max-width: 100vw;
    max-height: 100dvh;
    margin: 0;
    border: 0;
    padding: var(--gap);
    overflow: hidden;
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ground) 82%, transparent);
    /* Safari needed the prefix until 18. The design degrades to a plain tint
       without it, which is legible but not the effect. */
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(24px);
    backdrop-filter: blur(24px);
  }

  dialog.expanded::backdrop {
    background: transparent;
  }

  /* LOCK THE DOCUMENT WHILE THE VIEW IS OPEN. `showModal()` makes the rest of
     the page inert, but it does NOT stop it scrolling — measured here, not
     assumed: with the dialog open, one PageDown moved the document from 3233 to
     4020 while the image stayed put, so the visitor closes the view and finds
     themselves somewhere else on the page.

     `:has()` is the right tool and this is the case docs/styling.md permits it
     for: a state toggle, not structure. Nothing is laid out by this rule; it
     only stops something moving.

     It also removes the scrollbar, which makes the dialog's own `100vw` exact.
     While the bar is present `100vw` includes it, so the dialog would overhang
     by its width and put a horizontal scrollbar on the document underneath. One
     rule, both problems. */
  :global(html:has(dialog.expanded[open])) {
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* [open] rather than the bare selector: the UA rule is `dialog:not([open])
     { display: none }`, so declaring `display` unconditionally would leave the
     dialog on the page permanently. */
  dialog.expanded[open] {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s2);
    justify-items: end;
  }

  dialog.expanded img {
    justify-self: center;
    align-self: center;
    max-width: 100%;
    max-height: 100%;
    width: auto;
  }

  /* --ink, not the --muted that .t-spec supplies. This is the only control in
     the view, it sits over a translucent ground whose effective contrast is
     whatever image happens to be behind it, and 4.90:1 against a flat ground is
     not a number that survives that. --ink is 15.6:1 and survives anything. */
  .close {
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--s1) 0;
    border: 0;
    background: none;
    color: var(--ink);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  /* NO FADE. The view opens and closes instantly, which is what <dialog> does on
     its own — `showModal()` and `close()` with nothing layered over them.

     A 0.24s opacity transition used to sit here, and rebuilding it is not just a
     `transition` declaration: an element that goes `display: none` cannot
     animate without `allow-discrete` on `display` and `overlay`, plus an
     `@starting-style` block to give the opening frame something to start from.
     docs/motion.md keeps the working version.

     THE BLUR ABOVE IS NOT A FADE and stayed. It is a static effect that is
     either on or off with the dialog, and it is what makes this read as one
     Frame over the page rather than a page that was replaced. */
</style>
